\section*{Morava K- and E-theory 1 — Show that pi\_* L\_K(n) S\textasciicircum{}0 is finitely generated over the p-adics in each degree.}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
At every prime and positive height, each integer-degree homotopy group of the K(n)-local sphere is claimed to be finitely generated over Z\_p, equivalently for every localized finite p-local spectrum. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} At every prime $p$, positive height $n$ and integer degree $t$, OpenAI claims
\[
\pi_tL_{K(n)}S_p^\wedge\text{ is finitely generated over }\mathbb Z_p.
\]
Equivalently it claims the same for $\pi_tL_{K(n)}F$ for every finite $p$-local spectrum $F$. Here $K(n)$ is Morava $K$-theory, $L_{K(n)}$ its localization and $\mathbb Z_p$ the $p$-adic integers. Negative as well as nonnegative degrees are included.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 313, 2026-09-24): Finite division-point markings and compact-support geometry control stabilized transfer images in continuous Morava coefficient cohomology. Laurent-series models and Frobenius contraction remove a positive-order acyclic kernel, while Koszul extension arguments promote stable-image finiteness to the required coefficient groups. Sphere descent completes the module-finiteness conclusion, and finite cofiber sequences and retracts extend it to finite spectra.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 313, 2026-09-24): Theorem 1.1 is exactly integer-degree finite generation over the p-adic integers for every prime and positive height, and explicitly excludes the different Picard-graded extension mentioned in the background.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 313 has no Lean scope page in this snapshot; no formalization of these manuscript results is listed.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

